How do you know the main difference between human and transhuman won't
be the value system?

/I think that anything that delays the coming technology is bad, and 
\anything that accelerates it is good. At this point, extropianism gets
/a plus for raising the levels of awareness of transhumanity, and minuses
\for thinking that any one political agenda is the "answer" to the
/question of the proper stance toward the technology.

"Coming technology." Hmm.. let's think about current technology.
Like space. I've become convinced that one side in the political
debate (the statists) have been very detrimental to our development
of this technology. Look at, for instance, the fact that the only
advanced launcher program for roughly the past fifteen years,
SSRT, got only enough money to pay for 1/10th of a shuttle launch,
because Shuttle=government jobs= votes bought with your tax money.
SSRT, being a bit more commercializeable if successful, would end
that.

Not to mention The Great Protect Us From Proton Fiasco.

Proton rockets apparently take 1/3 the man hours to manufacture
as a comparable Ariane. The cost at which they can be sold in this
country is not as proportionately less. (And shouldn't it be even
less than that, after all, don't the Russians need the money? The
1/3 factor is only if the Russian workers get western wages, and
they don't, nor do they have to to maintain a western standard of
living at the present moment, although that will prob. change in a
couple more years).

Given just those two single examples, not to mention megaspending
megacolliders and tokamak monopolies, I suspect that politics and
technology are impacting each other at an alarming rate and something
has to be done about the politics before it's too late.

I think the Singularity is much farther off than everyone else on
this list thinks, as far as I can tell. The triumph of the current
sort of statism, I suspect, would probably delay it forever. I
don't want that to happen.

