From: Reilly Jones <70544.1227@compuserve.com>

>The rent-a-womb mentality is a slippery slope to dehumanization.  eople are not
>"products" and they don't "produce products" meaning children. I see no problem

I agree that people are valuable entities, but we are "products" of a long
evolutionary process.  If our intelligence has brought us to the point
where we can tinker with our own genetic structure ("our own" is key here,
since I'm not advocating force), then I think it is a natural, good thing.

>I took this idea of selling genes on the market to be selling whole fertilized
>eggs.  That would be weird.  

Is it 'weird' because of a personal distaste, or because of "moral, societal"
reasons? Personally, I don't find it weird at all. If I could make a lot of
money selling my fertilized eggs, I would do it in a minute. I think that
the idea is tremendously exciting. Imagine the diversity of people getting
into this area and the motivating factors that would drive people to be
smarter, faster, healthier etc!

>strange and expensive problems on their hands, the parents should not be allowed
>to walk away from the problem scot-free or dump the expenses onto the taxpayers
>shoulders.  There must be socially recognized responsibility for the expenses
>incurred of children in the form of legally binding requirements (basically a
>derivative of contract law).  Long-jump genetic tinkering has very unpredictable
>results and morally should only be undertaken by very wealthy parents to avoid
>making other people pay for their experimentation.  Society is not going to let
>people die without making an effort to help.  You cannot dissociate social
>values from individual values.

My alarm bells start going off when I hear the word "should" in the same
sentences as "socially recognized responsibility" and "social values".
I agree that some form of legally binding requirements are necessary
in the purchase of genetic material (whether it be ova, sperm whatever), but
you're walking on a slippery slope of socialism in your above paragraph.
"Society" is made up of individuals. I want to be honest, show integrity, do
what's responsible and good for *me*, not what's good for society.  So I
disagree with your statement that one cannot dissociate social values
from individual values.

