From: martino@gomez.jpl.nasa.gov (Martin R. Olah)
Subject: Oh Joy!

  Well, Kennita asked why I don't do the Gift Thing, so I'll give a quick and
  concentrated reply. Remember, these are my opinions only!

Gift giving at preordained occassions becomes programmed and unspontaneous,
devaluing the act. I give when it is more appropriate.

Gift receiving can bring feelings of obligation.

Gift givers often give what THEY would like, wasting their resources.

I don't have any more room for useless things.(this is not an exaggeration)

I don't know what most people want or need.

Most people don't know what I want or need.

I'd like EVERY day to be as special as the next, not based on the calendar.

It can encourage children to be greedy.(in the bad sense of the word)

I'm a long way from being rich, as are most people.

I don't like doing things just because everyone else does.

It can be hard to draw the line between who does and doesn't get gifts.

Those are some of the reasons that pop into mind. I've only been doing this for
a couple of years so I'm still working out the bugs. As you can imagine it is
very hard to go against the grain, and I constantly have to consider that I may
be hurting someone's feelings. Fortunately I have a life-long reputation for
being a little different, which makes this slightly easier. Another problem is
that I'm a great shopper and really enjoy giving people cool things.(but all of
the above still applies)

What I do as of 12-93:


I will bring a bottle of wine, etc. to a friendly gathering.

I will randomly buy something for someone if I know they would really like it.

I will get together on a proscribed date if there is no gift exchange or undue
mumbo-jumbo.(Thanksgiving,Halloween-no trick or treating of cours,New Years)

I will wish someone a Happy ______(whatever the occasion is)

I will reply to cards sent to me for any occasion.

Birthdays are still a problem because I may not get many opportunities to see
certain people otherwise. On the other hand, that's really my fault.
Thanksgiving was added back this year because it doesn't really contradict my
feelings, unless you go out of your way to make a religious connection.
Easter would be harder to justify, but at least gift giving isn't that strong
a part of it. As I said, I'm still working things out. All that I want is to
live my life based on MY principles rather than other's, yet not overly damage
my relationships to those who are important to me. Not an easy task.

                              - O Martin

