From: cards@top.cis.syr.edu
Subject: Question on Great Genetics Debate

I believe that genes are NOT atomic, BUT they often appear so
because breaks (in the normal course of sexual reproduction)
tend to occur more often in the (generally assumed passive)
'introns' which make up more of the total length of most
human chromosones than do the genes themselves.  It is not
clear if this is merely a consequence of the preponderance of
introns over genes, or if the process of myosis specifically
cuts the chromosones preferentially in the introns.  Perhaps
a more knowledgeable person might clarify this?
